WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:02.140
-(APPLAUSE)
-Yes. (CHUCKLES)

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.800
Uh, Andrew Ross Sorkin,
do you agree with Larry Kudlow

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.640
that Medicare
and Social Security should be

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.360
handed to Wall Street
through privatization,
is that what he's for?

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:12.378
-I'm sure he is.
-I'm sure he is for it.

00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:13.679
-BILL: Yeah.
-I don't think I'm for that.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:16.148
No, I'm gonna take
the other side of that.

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:17.717
Is that where you want me to go?

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:20.386
Well, I don't know.
What are you trying to
check in with me for?

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:21.887
I'm asking you the question.

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:24.557
-No, I think that's--
-BILL: I mean,
Bush proposed this.

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:25.858
I think that's a real problem.

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:27.960
I think the privatization of
so many things actually--

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.063
Especially when it comes to
people's pensions
and retirement,

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:31.664
part of our problem
has been that.

00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.434
So, I'm gonna
take the other side.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.870
-But as an economist,
only I'm not...
-ANDREW: Who plays one on TV.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.572
...I'm just gonna give you
the other argument.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:40.706
ANDREW: Okay, go for it.

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:43.342
I've seen the chart of where
money... (CHUCKLES)

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:45.277
If you put your money
in the stock market,

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:47.780
versus if you just put it
in the bank or whatever it is...

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.315
ANDREW: Yes, it'll do better
in the market.

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:50.883
...the market one
goes like this.

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.053
Now, you can't watch it
every year because the market
goes up and down,

00:00:54.153 --> 00:00:56.789
but over the time,
if you put it in
when you were 20,

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:01.494
by the time you retired at 65,
the money goes like this and--

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:05.365
-If we had a forced
savings program, by the way...
-BILL: I'm just saying.

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.633
If we had real
forced savings program,
where you couldn't take it out

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.971
and was effectively gonna be
managed elsewhere
and there was guarantees on it,

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:13.206
I could actually go for that.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:14.673
Yeah, it's not
the craziest idea...

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:15.942
ANDREW: Not the craziest idea
in the world.

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:20.479
...that Bush-- Bush, hey.
Who is he to you?
Is he your uncle? George Bush.

00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:21.647
-W?
-BILL: Yeah.

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:23.249
He's my, uh-- He's my cousin.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:24.550
BILL:
You had to think about that.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.686
Yeah, there's a lot of us there,
cousins.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.454
-Yeah, his dad's my uncle,
I'm pretty sure of it.
-BILL: Okay.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.924
(LAUGHING)

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:32.225
PETE: You guys hang out?

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:36.029
Uh, there's a lot of us, no,
not much.

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:40.800
So, Billy, "How is the rest of
the Bush family holding up
under Trump," this card says.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.301
Trying to take the high road,
I think.

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:43.469
-I don't know if he's--
-Right.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.638
I'm not sure either one
cast a vote for him.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.440
In fact,
I'm pretty sure they didn't.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:53.579
Speaking of Me Too stuff, boy,
the old man sure skated
on the ass grabbing.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.680
(LAUGHING)

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.881
Have you ever talked
to him about that?

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:58.885
I told him, "Never again,
you hear me? Don't do that." No.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:01:59.986
(LAUGHS)

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.422
But-- Because there's
a conservative, I mean,

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:05.357
sounds like the same thing
that Al Franken was accused of

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:08.928
and that just kind of, like,
went into the ether. Okay.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:12.598
Except he's a former president
of the United States who,

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:14.701
you know,
people didn't know that about.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.436
And there's a whole bunch
of women that've said that,
it's pretty messed up.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:18.538
Pretty unacceptable.

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:21.574
And there was a joke
that went with it,
it's disgusting and inexcusable.

00:02:21.908 --> 00:02:23.242
BILL: Right you are.

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:25.411
These people--
What a lot of fun you are.

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:26.746
Well, I mean, he has a...

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.213
I mean... Okay.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.216
ANDREW: There's no upside, yeah,
I'm with you.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.886
I'm not sure
what Al Franken did.
I'll just say that.

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:36.455
I'm talking about Pappy,
either one, I mean,

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:40.093
who's grabbing people like that
in a photo op? It's weird.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.494
It is weird, esp-- And--

00:02:42.594 --> 00:02:46.165
Everybody wants a photo with
Bill Maher,
have you ever even considered--

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:47.567
I don't even touch people.

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.568
-BILL:
I take pictures like this.
-Yes, exactly.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:50.670
(LAUGHING)

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.037
Every picture
I take is like this.

00:02:52.137 --> 00:02:53.505
-Exactly, yeah.
-Hands up.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.141
(LAUGHING)

00:02:56.241 --> 00:03:01.681
Okay. "Why does the NRA
get a tax exemption
and how do we take it away?"

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:05.451
(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:06.652
PETE: Elect Democrats.

00:03:06.752 --> 00:03:08.621
That's a good--
Because they do run--

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.889
They would like to say that
they don't run
a political action committee,

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:13.225
but they do have that wing,
otherwise they say they're

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:17.797
a grassroots non-profit
organization, but they use legal
corporate tax loopholes

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:22.268
to build out an entire
political lobbying wing
that doesn't get taxed.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.136
So that's
a corporate tax loophole,

00:03:24.236 --> 00:03:26.339
but that's also because
the NRA knows how
to work the system.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.941
BILL: Boy, do they.
And they know
how to use a crisis.

00:03:29.374 --> 00:03:31.610
They never let
a crisis go to waste.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.045
If you follow the money
on the NRA--

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:34.647
BILL: Now we're arming teachers.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:35.748
PETE: Not yet.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.083
BILL:
They somehow turn that around--

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:38.418
NAYYERA: You know, one shot
a student the other day.

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:39.852
Sure, it's moving ahead
in many states.

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:43.589
Somehow they turn that around
to a way they can
sell more guns.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:46.892
It's moving ahead in the States
where they still
beat kids with paddles.

00:03:47.192 --> 00:03:49.595
I mean, it's not--
You know, I don't think
it's taking hold.

00:03:49.828 --> 00:03:54.033
Certainly it's not--
There's no agreement amongst
educators and experts

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:56.569
about arming teachers,
teachers don't want that.

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.570
ANDREW: One thing that
I was just gonna say on money
on the NRA,

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:01.040
you know, you can go after them
on the taxes or whatever it is,

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.242
but the real thing,
the real opportunity,

00:04:03.342 --> 00:04:06.145
given I cover
the world of finance,
is the companies.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:08.848
It's all the companies that
do business with the NRA

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:10.449
and people who
do business with guns,

00:04:10.549 --> 00:04:13.652
it's the credit card companies
that allow the transactions
on the cards,

00:04:13.752 --> 00:04:15.721
it's Apple TV, and Amazon,

00:04:15.821 --> 00:04:19.057
and everybody who allows
this stuff over their airwaves,
that's--

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.159
(APPLAUSE)

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:22.394
Washington, unfortunately,
is not gonna do anything,
I don't think,

00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:24.763
anytime soon, I could be wrong,
I hope I'm wrong,

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:28.333
but I think in the meantime,
there's a huge swath of stuff
that can actually happen,

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:30.435
but it depends on everyone
actually doing something
about it.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:32.138
And that goes for the action
related to your pocket book,
right?

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:34.474
Consumers have to wait
about two years before we can

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:36.809
use our power as citizens
to kick somebody out of office,

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.145
I can make the decision tomorrow
if I want to go
to a different store.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:41.948
If I don't like-- Like Walmart,
Walmart, um--

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.382
They getting your business?

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:47.853
Uh, they got the business of
a lot of people out of that day.
Right? (LAUGHS)

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:49.388
But Dick's Sporting Goods did.

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:50.590
-BILL: Yeah.
-NAYYERA: Mm-hmm.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.658
-BILL: I got-- I, uh...
-(SCATTERED APPLAUSE)

00:04:52.758 --> 00:04:54.360
I got paddled a couple of times.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.096
-NAYYERA: Hey now.
-Didn't seem to destroy me.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:05:01.401
You know, but...
Only when you do something
really bad, you know...

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.436
-You know--
-NAYYERA:
I wasn't sure what kind of

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:04.704
paddle you were talking about,
Bill.

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:05.972
Didn't have nails in it
or anything. It was--

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.941
I'm not quite sure what paddling
he's talking about,
but maybe we should move on.

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:10.543
(LAUGHING)

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:12.311
I'm just saying.

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:15.614
Uh, "If you worked
in the White House,

00:05:15.714 --> 00:05:19.919
would you resign on principle
or stay to help minimize
the damage Trump causes?"

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:23.189
Oh, so, I, um-- I don't think
there's any heroism in

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.757
serving in this White House
anymore.

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:27.392
I think initially people really
did think that--

00:05:27.492 --> 00:05:30.829
Like John Kelly, he was like,
"I'm gonna serve my country
and do this."

00:05:30.929 --> 00:05:34.534
But it's, um--
You're not serving the public
at this point, right?

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:37.937
You're serving Trump
and his personal Trump interests

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:39.238
and that's just not--

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:40.773
PETE: And you don't know what
they are.

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.909
Right, but it's actually
very clear what they are,

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:46.745
they're advancing his family
and whoever else is willing to
line his pocket books,

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:49.248
whether it be Russian oligarchs
or any other dictators

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:51.049
that he may
or may not want to emulate.

00:05:51.149 --> 00:05:54.753
But there is-- And yes,
I did work in the White House

00:05:54.853 --> 00:05:57.489
and there is absolutely
no question that I would ever

00:05:57.589 --> 00:05:59.559
work in a Trump White House
and the sad thing is

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:01.760
everybody who's walked out
has walked out tarnished.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.029
Like, it's a dead end job now.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:05.598
Icarus, fly close to the sun.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.799
-NAYYERA: Right.
-Yes.

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:10.937
Monica Lewinsky has a better
reputation than
Sean Spicer does.

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:12.038
BILL: Who does?

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:15.007
Monica Lewinsky is doing better
brand-wise than Sean Spicer,

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:16.408
the former
White House press secretary.

00:06:16.508 --> 00:06:18.711
No, anybody, as you can attest,
anybody who gets in contact,

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.446
he's a human wood chipper,
people just--

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.650
Everything he touches dies!

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:28.154
If you don't have the courage
to say no to a job
to Donald Trump,

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:31.424
you shouldn't be running
a McDonald's,
much less a White House.

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:32.625
But we have a problem.

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:35.094
I do wanna throw in
about public, like,
public servants who are--

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:36.395
I was a political appointee,

00:06:36.495 --> 00:06:37.996
there are public servants
and the foreign service,

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:41.466
the military, civil servants,
who keep the day to day function
of government running,

00:06:41.566 --> 00:06:44.703
the fact that they are feeling
demoralized is a massive problem

00:06:44.803 --> 00:06:46.805
because they're the ones who
make sure we, you know,

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:48.274
get things like our
social security checks.

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:50.909
All I was gonna say is,
the real problem though is if--

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.678
Look, you can call all
the people who showed up

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:54.680
in the beginning opportunists,
which they are.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.582
Today,
the new people are believers,

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.117
and fortunately
or unfortunately,

00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:00.085
but fortunately for Trump
and unfortunately maybe
for everybody else.

00:07:00.185 --> 00:07:01.287
PETE:
Sycophants and cartoon villains.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.556
But the opportunists,
to the degree you thought that

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:05.658
they were saving the country
from Trump,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:07.693
those people
don't exist anymore.

00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:11.164
And so I think there has to be
some thought about

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:14.333
whether you do want some
diversity of thought
in the room.

00:07:14.433 --> 00:07:17.303
Even if it's gonna taint people,
I don't know what
the right answer is.

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:19.438
He has to want the diversity
of thought in the room.

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:20.405
But he doesn't.

00:07:20.739 --> 00:07:22.275
Exactly, so, like, what do you
get out of serving?

00:07:22.375 --> 00:07:24.310
-How are you gonna fix anything?
-PETE: He doesn't want
any thought in the room.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.479
But that's what they're claiming
these days, all over TV,

00:07:27.579 --> 00:07:28.581
is that he w--

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:31.017
That's why there's
this turnover, he has this--

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.419
He says he likes to see people
go at it in a fight.

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:36.421
And I'm like, "This is the guy
who's going to negotiate with
Kim Jong Un?"

00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:38.357
Like... (GRUNTS)
"I like to see people go at it."

00:07:38.457 --> 00:07:39.992
BILLY: This is how we know him,
the boardroom.

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:44.129
He's the host of The Apprentice,
"You're fired", he instigates.

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:47.600
They're all about selling
lemonade on a corner
in 5th Avenue--

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.069
It wasn't like it was all there
for us to judge.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:53.739
But he made voters think he'd
be able to take business savvy

00:07:53.839 --> 00:07:57.309
and apply that to politics
and all these other things
and policy--

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.912
So many people are complicit
in that though, don't you think?

00:08:00.012 --> 00:08:01.547
It is, and it's shady business
deals,

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:02.882
that's the problem,
it works in real estate,

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.850
but it doesn't work in diplomacy
or policy.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.852
How many of those voters that
he convinces--
This is my question.

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:08.654
How many of those voters
that he did convince,

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:10.656
uh, you know, have changed
their mind?

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:14.693
Or more importantly,
has anybody said,
since Trump was elected,

00:08:14.793 --> 00:08:17.529
"You know, I was really against
him the whole time,

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:20.332
but in these 14 months
he's really impressed me."

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:21.534
(LAUGHING)

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:24.303
"I think I'm gonna reconsider
my vote for November."

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.138
That's, like, one guy who's
a pig farmer.

00:08:26.238 --> 00:08:27.439
I think there are.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:28.540
Really?

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.309
Thank you, everybody.
Thank you, panel.

00:08:30.542 --> 00:08:32.011
(CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)

